(5238767, 760286, 67697, '<td valign="top">I loved this idea and thought it was a perfect concept for the silence challenge but as some others mentioned, it was the technicals that ended up hurting the photo.  I too thought you could have pulled yourself away from the wall since the camera actually ended up focusing on it instead of you.  One thing to think about trying is getting a desk lamp or some kind of spotlight and have it focus on the area of just your hands and mouth.  Then turn off the lights and use maybe an ISO of 200.  The idea would be to take the attention away from your face and bring more focus to your hands.  Just a thought for you - good luck in the next challenge!</td>', 'I loved this idea and thought it was a perfect concept for the silence challenge but as some others mentioned, it was the technicals that ended up hurting the photo.  I too thought you could have pulled yourself away from the wall since the camera actually ended up focusing on it instead of you.  One thing to think about trying is getting a desk lamp or some kind of spotlight and have it focus on the area of just your hands and mouth.  Then turn off the lights and use maybe an ISO of 200.  The idea would be to take the attention away from your face and bring more focus to your hands.  Just a thought for you - good luck in the next challenge!', '2009-02-04 08:34:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(5238773, 759107, 63218, '<td valign="top">Hello Jan, I am one of your 10 votes. This is technical perfection, but that is not what motivated my score. It was the feeling of Silence. The inward-turning silence that makes me conscious of how much noise my life makes &amp; at the same times connects me to everything that is wonderful &amp; unknowable. I like the layers of light, too, it seems to get more heavenly nearer the windows (must be why church ceilings are so high).  I have never tried this kind of shot, but it looks fearsomely difficult. In its genre, I think it would compare very well, your church-interior photog peers would be proud of you.  I don''t comment during voting because I like to get a sense of who the photog is before I risk a comment.  Head up, tail up, &amp; keep on clickin''!</td>', 'Hello Jan, I am one of your 10 votes. This is technical perfection, but that is not what motivated my score. It was the feeling of Silence. The inward-turning silence that makes me conscious of how much noise my life makes & at the same times connects me to everything that is wonderful & unknowable. I like the layers of light, too, it seems to get more heavenly nearer the windows (must be why church ceilings are so high).  I have never tried this kind of shot, but it looks fearsomely difficult. In its genre, I think it would compare very well, your church-interior photog peers would be proud of you.  I don''t comment during voting because I like to get a sense of who the photog is before I risk a comment.  Head up, tail up, & keep on clickin''!', '2009-02-04 08:39:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(5238787, 760051, 43410, '<td valign="top">good shot clay, i think the comments below are spot on.  only thing lacking in this is composition.  i think you were going for a "through the bushes" type vantage point, and if so you got it, but stepping back and getting a "not so tight" crop of this would have upped the score a little.  but, that being said you''re consistently getting high votes, if that''s what you''re after. :)<br/> <br/>keep shooting, good stuff</td>', 'good shot clay, i think the comments below are spot on.  only thing lacking in this is composition.  i think you were going for a "through the bushes" type vantage point, and if so you got it, but stepping back and getting a "not so tight" crop of this would have upped the score a little.  but, that being said you''re consistently getting high votes, if that''s what you''re after. :) keep shooting, good stuff', '2009-02-04 08:50:51.000000', NULL, 0),
